1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,749
Under the sea between England and France,

2
00:00:02,749 --> 00:00:05,029
there is a machine that will never

3
00:00:05,029 --> 00:00:06,304
see daylight again.

4
00:00:06,484 --> 00:00:08,894
It dug part of the Channel Tunnel,

5
00:00:08,894 --> 00:00:10,737
and when the job was done,

6
00:00:10,737 --> 00:00:13,148
the workers did not bring it back.

7
00:00:13,328 --> 00:00:15,646
They steered it down into the rock,

8
00:00:15,646 --> 00:00:16,640
out of the way,

9
00:00:16,640 --> 00:00:17,833
and left it there.

10
00:00:18,013 --> 00:00:21,078
Today, eleven parts of a tunnel boring

11
00:00:21,078 --> 00:00:23,740
machine, and why each one exists,

12
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:26,967
starting with the strange reason so many

13
00:00:26,967 --> 00:00:28,984
of them stay underground.

14
00:00:29,334 --> 00:00:32,181
A tunnel boring machine is a factory

15
00:00:32,181 --> 00:00:33,130
on the move.

16
00:00:33,310 --> 00:00:35,963
The front is a spinning wheel of

17
00:00:35,963 --> 00:00:39,196
steel that grinds through rock or soil.

18
00:00:39,376 --> 00:00:42,417
Behind it, the machine builds the tunnel

19
00:00:42,417 --> 00:00:43,710
walls as it goes,

20
00:00:43,710 --> 00:00:44,926
and behind that,

21
00:00:44,926 --> 00:00:47,511
a train of trailers up to hundreds

22
00:00:47,511 --> 00:00:50,704
of meters long carries everything the crew

23
00:00:50,704 --> 00:00:51,160
needs.

24
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:54,129
Some of these machines are wider than

25
00:00:54,129 --> 00:00:56,391
a four story building is tall.

26
00:00:56,740 --> 00:00:59,512
As always, every fact comes from a

27
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:01,957
named source, tunnel builders,

28
00:01:01,957 --> 00:01:06,277
engineering references, and official project reports,

29
00:01:06,277 --> 00:01:08,722
all listed in the description.

30
00:01:08,902 --> 00:01:10,882
Let's start with the question in the

31
00:01:10,882 --> 00:01:11,212
title.

32
00:01:11,562 --> 00:01:14,597
Why would anyone bury a machine worth

33
00:01:14,597 --> 00:01:15,336
millions?

34
00:01:15,516 --> 00:01:17,674
Because it cannot go back the way

35
00:01:17,674 --> 00:01:18,198
it came.

36
00:01:18,378 --> 00:01:20,896
The front of a tunnel boring machine,

37
00:01:20,896 --> 00:01:22,121
called the shield,

38
00:01:22,121 --> 00:01:24,502
is a little wider than the finished

39
00:01:24,502 --> 00:01:25,659
tunnel behind it,

40
00:01:25,659 --> 00:01:28,450
because the tunnel walls are built inside

41
00:01:28,450 --> 00:01:29,198
the shield.

42
00:01:29,378 --> 00:01:31,798
So the machine cannot reverse through the

43
00:01:31,798 --> 00:01:32,919
tunnel it just dug.

44
00:01:33,099 --> 00:01:34,919
It can only go forward,

45
00:01:34,919 --> 00:01:37,294
or be cut into pieces where it

46
00:01:37,294 --> 00:01:37,848
stands.

47
00:01:38,198 --> 00:01:41,058
Cutting it up underground is slow and

48
00:01:41,058 --> 00:01:41,831
expensive.

49
00:01:42,011 --> 00:01:44,748
Workers need weeks or months to take

50
00:01:44,748 --> 00:01:47,484
the machine apart in a narrow tunnel

51
00:01:47,484 --> 00:01:49,384
and carry the pieces out.

52
00:01:49,564 --> 00:01:52,383
Often the used machine is worth less

53
00:01:52,383 --> 00:01:53,558
than that work.

54
00:01:53,738 --> 00:01:55,822
So when the end of the tunnel

55
00:01:55,822 --> 00:01:57,260
is not near a shaft,

56
00:01:57,260 --> 00:02:00,135
builders sometimes do the cheaper thing.

57
00:02:00,315 --> 00:02:02,654
They strip out the useful parts,

58
00:02:02,654 --> 00:02:05,287
drive the shield into a side pocket,

59
00:02:05,287 --> 00:02:07,188
and fill it with concrete.

60
00:02:07,538 --> 00:02:09,830
That is what happened under the English

61
00:02:09,830 --> 00:02:10,301
Channel.

62
00:02:10,481 --> 00:02:13,111
The British and French machines met in

63
00:02:13,111 --> 00:02:14,219
nineteen ninety.

64
00:02:14,399 --> 00:02:16,855
The British ones turned down into the

65
00:02:16,855 --> 00:02:18,249
rock and were buried,

66
00:02:18,249 --> 00:02:20,771
so the French ones could break through

67
00:02:20,771 --> 00:02:21,899
and be taken out.

68
00:02:22,079 --> 00:02:25,426
Years later, one Channel Tunnel machine was

69
00:02:25,426 --> 00:02:27,605
even put up for sale online.

70
00:02:27,785 --> 00:02:31,169
Many machines today are refurbished and reused,

71
00:02:31,169 --> 00:02:33,544
but some still end their lives as

72
00:02:33,544 --> 00:02:35,703
part of the tunnel they built.

73
00:02:36,054 --> 00:02:38,000
Why is the front of a tunnel

74
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:41,197
boring machine covered in little steel wheels?

75
00:02:41,377 --> 00:02:43,502
Because they do not cut the rock

76
00:02:43,502 --> 00:02:44,233
like a saw.

77
00:02:44,413 --> 00:02:46,581
Each disc rolls in a circle on

78
00:02:46,581 --> 00:02:47,593
the rock face,

79
00:02:47,593 --> 00:02:50,485
pressed against it with tonnes of force.

80
00:02:50,665 --> 00:02:53,698
The pressure crushes the rock under the

81
00:02:53,698 --> 00:02:55,876
disc and cracks it sideways,

82
00:02:55,876 --> 00:02:59,454
so chips flake off between neighboring tracks.

83
00:02:59,634 --> 00:03:01,305
The rock breaks itself.

84
00:03:01,656 --> 00:03:04,438
A big hard rock machine can carry

85
00:03:04,438 --> 00:03:06,294
dozens of these discs,

86
00:03:06,294 --> 00:03:09,330
each about forty centimeters across.

87
00:03:09,510 --> 00:03:12,137
The cutterhead turns a few times per

88
00:03:12,137 --> 00:03:15,129
minute, pushed forward by hydraulic rams.

89
00:03:15,309 --> 00:03:16,824
In very hard rock,

90
00:03:16,824 --> 00:03:20,021
like granite, the discs wear out fast,

91
00:03:20,021 --> 00:03:23,303
and the crew stops regularly to inspect

92
00:03:23,303 --> 00:03:25,828
them and change the worn ones.

93
00:03:26,178 --> 00:03:28,562
Changing them is one of the hardest

94
00:03:28,562 --> 00:03:29,788
jobs in tunneling.

95
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:32,593
Workers climb through the cutterhead to the

96
00:03:32,593 --> 00:03:33,753
face of the tunnel.

97
00:03:33,933 --> 00:03:36,017
In soft ground under water,

98
00:03:36,017 --> 00:03:39,260
the chamber behind the cutterhead is under

99
00:03:39,260 --> 00:03:42,888
pressure, so the workers breathe compressed air

100
00:03:42,888 --> 00:03:45,512
and need decompression afterwards,

101
00:03:45,512 --> 00:03:47,134
like deep sea divers.

102
00:03:47,314 --> 00:03:50,446
Some new machines change discs with robots

103
00:03:50,446 --> 00:03:52,236
for exactly that reason.

104
00:03:52,586 --> 00:03:54,494
Why is the wall of the tunnel

105
00:03:54,494 --> 00:03:56,534
made of curved concrete pieces?

106
00:03:56,714 --> 00:03:59,878
Because the machine builds the tunnel as

107
00:03:59,878 --> 00:04:00,510
it digs.

108
00:04:00,690 --> 00:04:02,988
The pieces, called segments,

109
00:04:02,988 --> 00:04:05,696
are made in a factory and brought

110
00:04:05,696 --> 00:04:06,681
in by train.

111
00:04:06,861 --> 00:04:08,490
Inside the shield,

112
00:04:08,490 --> 00:04:12,019
a rotating arm called the erector picks

113
00:04:12,019 --> 00:04:14,734
up each segment and places it,

114
00:04:14,734 --> 00:04:16,091
forming a ring.

115
00:04:16,271 --> 00:04:19,315
Usually six or seven segments plus a

116
00:04:19,315 --> 00:04:21,937
small key piece close one ring.

117
00:04:22,286 --> 00:04:25,172
The rings are bolted together and sealed

118
00:04:25,172 --> 00:04:26,615
with rubber gaskets,

119
00:04:26,615 --> 00:04:29,141
so water from the ground cannot get

120
00:04:29,141 --> 00:04:29,357
in.

121
00:04:29,537 --> 00:04:32,571
The machine digs a little more than

122
00:04:32,571 --> 00:04:33,958
one ring length,

123
00:04:33,958 --> 00:04:36,386
stops, builds the next ring,

124
00:04:36,386 --> 00:04:37,513
and moves on.

125
00:04:37,693 --> 00:04:38,931
Ring after ring,

126
00:04:38,931 --> 00:04:41,870
the tunnel appears behind the machine,

127
00:04:41,870 --> 00:04:43,185
already finished.

128
00:04:43,534 --> 00:04:44,920
The last segment,

129
00:04:44,920 --> 00:04:47,528
the key, is shaped like a wedge.

130
00:04:47,708 --> 00:04:50,015
It slides in last and locks the

131
00:04:50,015 --> 00:04:52,544
whole ring, like the keystone of a

132
00:04:52,544 --> 00:04:53,362
stone arch.

133
00:04:53,542 --> 00:04:55,606
Without it, the ring is just a

134
00:04:55,606 --> 00:04:57,258
pile of curved concrete.

135
00:04:57,438 --> 00:04:59,381
With it, the ring can hold back

136
00:04:59,381 --> 00:05:01,387
the weight of the ground and the

137
00:05:01,387 --> 00:05:03,456
pressure of water for more than a

138
00:05:03,456 --> 00:05:04,334
hundred years.

139
00:05:04,683 --> 00:05:07,229
How does a machine without wheels move

140
00:05:07,229 --> 00:05:09,037
forward through solid rock?

141
00:05:09,217 --> 00:05:11,785
It pushes against the tunnel it just

142
00:05:11,785 --> 00:05:12,213
built.

143
00:05:12,393 --> 00:05:14,787
Around the back of the shield,

144
00:05:14,787 --> 00:05:18,138
a ring of powerful hydraulic jacks presses

145
00:05:18,138 --> 00:05:20,612
against the last concrete ring.

146
00:05:20,792 --> 00:05:22,034
When they extend,

147
00:05:22,034 --> 00:05:24,955
the whole machine slides forward and the

148
00:05:24,955 --> 00:05:27,584
cutterhead is pressed into the face.

149
00:05:27,764 --> 00:05:29,732
Then the jacks pull back,

150
00:05:29,732 --> 00:05:31,305
a new ring is built,

151
00:05:31,305 --> 00:05:33,430
and the cycle starts again.

152
00:05:33,779 --> 00:05:35,693
The force can be enormous,

153
00:05:35,693 --> 00:05:38,194
tens of thousands of tonnes on the

154
00:05:38,194 --> 00:05:39,445
biggest machines.

155
00:05:39,625 --> 00:05:41,680
And the jacks also steer.

156
00:05:41,860 --> 00:05:43,845
Push harder on the left side,

157
00:05:43,845 --> 00:05:45,761
and the machine turns right.

158
00:05:45,941 --> 00:05:48,107
Push harder at the bottom,

159
00:05:48,107 --> 00:05:49,273
and it climbs.

160
00:05:49,453 --> 00:05:51,892
The operator does this constantly,

161
00:05:51,892 --> 00:05:54,761
guided by lasers and computers that know

162
00:05:54,761 --> 00:05:57,415
where the machine should be to within

163
00:05:57,415 --> 00:05:58,706
a few millimeters.

164
00:05:59,056 --> 00:06:00,014
In hard rock,

165
00:06:00,014 --> 00:06:02,667
some machines use a different trick.

166
00:06:02,847 --> 00:06:05,207
Instead of pushing on segments,

167
00:06:05,207 --> 00:06:08,479
they press big pads called grippers against

168
00:06:08,479 --> 00:06:10,686
the rock walls on both sides,

169
00:06:10,686 --> 00:06:12,589
lock themselves in place,

170
00:06:12,589 --> 00:06:15,862
and push the cutterhead forward from there.

171
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:18,375
Then they release the grippers,

172
00:06:18,375 --> 00:06:20,031
pull the body forward,

173
00:06:20,031 --> 00:06:21,160
and grip again,

174
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:22,439
like an inchworm.

175
00:06:22,789 --> 00:06:25,155
Why does a tunnel boring machine pump

176
00:06:25,155 --> 00:06:27,712
liquid grout behind the walls it builds?

177
00:06:27,892 --> 00:06:29,610
Because there is a gap.

178
00:06:29,790 --> 00:06:32,689
The cutterhead digs a hole slightly wider

179
00:06:32,689 --> 00:06:33,820
than the shield,

180
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:36,436
and the segments are built inside the

181
00:06:36,436 --> 00:06:36,931
shield.

182
00:06:37,111 --> 00:06:39,007
When the shield moves on,

183
00:06:39,007 --> 00:06:41,207
a ring of empty space is left

184
00:06:41,207 --> 00:06:43,937
between the concrete and the ground.

185
00:06:44,117 --> 00:06:45,586
If nothing fills it,

186
00:06:45,586 --> 00:06:48,378
the ground above slowly sinks into it.

187
00:06:48,727 --> 00:06:49,520
In a city,

188
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:51,026
that matters a lot.

189
00:06:51,206 --> 00:06:54,804
A few centimeters of settlement can crack

190
00:06:54,804 --> 00:06:56,559
the buildings above,

191
00:06:56,559 --> 00:06:58,139
break water pipes,

192
00:06:58,139 --> 00:07:00,158
or tilt railway tracks.

193
00:07:00,338 --> 00:07:02,988
So the machine injects grout through the

194
00:07:02,988 --> 00:07:04,247
tail of the shield,

195
00:07:04,247 --> 00:07:06,633
at the same moment it moves forward,

196
00:07:06,633 --> 00:07:09,085
filling the gap before the ground can

197
00:07:09,085 --> 00:07:09,417
move.

198
00:07:09,766 --> 00:07:12,739
Engineers watch the surface the whole time.

199
00:07:12,919 --> 00:07:16,283
Under London, during the Crossrail project,

200
00:07:16,283 --> 00:07:20,118
thousands of sensors measured buildings above the

201
00:07:20,118 --> 00:07:20,744
tunnels.

202
00:07:20,924 --> 00:07:22,997
Under some historic buildings,

203
00:07:22,997 --> 00:07:25,623
they even pumped grout into the ground

204
00:07:25,623 --> 00:07:28,111
beforehand, ready to push it back up

205
00:07:28,111 --> 00:07:29,631
if it started to sink.

206
00:07:29,981 --> 00:07:32,720
Why do soft ground tunnel machines keep

207
00:07:32,720 --> 00:07:34,968
a chamber full of mud behind the

208
00:07:34,968 --> 00:07:35,740
cutterhead?

209
00:07:35,920 --> 00:07:38,675
Because in soft soil under water,

210
00:07:38,675 --> 00:07:41,264
the face of the tunnel wants to

211
00:07:41,264 --> 00:07:42,015
collapse.

212
00:07:42,195 --> 00:07:44,696
The soil and water push into the

213
00:07:44,696 --> 00:07:45,086
hole.

214
00:07:45,266 --> 00:07:48,462
An earth pressure balance machine fills the

215
00:07:48,462 --> 00:07:51,584
chamber behind the cutterhead with the dug

216
00:07:51,584 --> 00:07:54,483
soil itself, turned into a thick paste,

217
00:07:54,483 --> 00:07:57,307
and keeps it pressed against the face,

218
00:07:57,307 --> 00:07:59,834
at the same pressure as the ground

219
00:07:59,834 --> 00:08:00,429
outside.

220
00:08:00,779 --> 00:08:03,384
A long screw conveyor takes the paste

221
00:08:03,384 --> 00:08:04,722
out of the chamber,

222
00:08:04,722 --> 00:08:07,257
and its speed controls the pressure.

223
00:08:07,437 --> 00:08:08,692
Turn it faster,

224
00:08:08,692 --> 00:08:10,281
and pressure drops.

225
00:08:10,461 --> 00:08:11,906
Turn it slower,

226
00:08:11,906 --> 00:08:13,735
and pressure rises.

227
00:08:13,915 --> 00:08:15,787
If the pressure is too low,

228
00:08:15,787 --> 00:08:18,422
the ground above can collapse into the

229
00:08:18,422 --> 00:08:18,908
tunnel.

230
00:08:19,088 --> 00:08:20,543
If it is too high,

231
00:08:20,543 --> 00:08:23,374
the machine can push the ground up,

232
00:08:23,374 --> 00:08:25,639
and even blow mud out at the

233
00:08:25,639 --> 00:08:26,286
surface.

234
00:08:26,636 --> 00:08:29,810
Other machines, called slurry shields,

235
00:08:29,810 --> 00:08:33,485
use bentonite clay mixed with water instead.

236
00:08:33,665 --> 00:08:35,911
The slurry holds the face and is

237
00:08:35,911 --> 00:08:38,297
pumped through pipes to a plant on

238
00:08:38,297 --> 00:08:41,104
the surface, where the soil is separated

239
00:08:41,104 --> 00:08:43,139
out and the slurry is reused.

240
00:08:43,319 --> 00:08:46,326
Either way, the idea is the same.

241
00:08:46,506 --> 00:08:49,341
Hold the ground with pressure until the

242
00:08:49,341 --> 00:08:51,231
concrete ring is in place.

243
00:08:51,581 --> 00:08:54,141
Why does a tunnel boring machine drag

244
00:08:54,141 --> 00:08:56,562
a long chain of trailers behind it?

245
00:08:56,742 --> 00:08:59,505
Because the cutterhead is only the tip.

246
00:08:59,685 --> 00:09:02,326
Behind it, on what is called the

247
00:09:02,326 --> 00:09:05,462
backup, are the electric transformers,

248
00:09:05,462 --> 00:09:07,855
hydraulic pumps, grout tanks,

249
00:09:07,855 --> 00:09:10,248
ventilation, a control cabin,

250
00:09:10,248 --> 00:09:12,889
toilets and a place for the crew

251
00:09:12,889 --> 00:09:13,466
to eat.

252
00:09:13,646 --> 00:09:15,456
The whole thing can be more than

253
00:09:15,456 --> 00:09:16,700
a hundred meters long.

254
00:09:17,049 --> 00:09:19,766
The dug material has to leave too.

255
00:09:19,946 --> 00:09:21,219
On many machines,

256
00:09:21,219 --> 00:09:24,140
a conveyor belt carries the broken rock

257
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:25,937
back through the tunnel,

258
00:09:25,937 --> 00:09:27,809
sometimes for kilometers,

259
00:09:27,809 --> 00:09:28,932
to the surface.

260
00:09:29,112 --> 00:09:31,938
On others, small trains carry it out

261
00:09:31,938 --> 00:09:34,842
in wagons, and bring segments back in

262
00:09:34,842 --> 00:09:36,334
on the return trip.

263
00:09:36,683 --> 00:09:39,992
The crew lives inside this moving factory.

264
00:09:40,172 --> 00:09:42,432
Work goes on day and night,

265
00:09:42,432 --> 00:09:44,189
in shifts, for years.

266
00:09:44,369 --> 00:09:45,541
On a good day,

267
00:09:45,541 --> 00:09:48,555
a modern machine can advance tens of

268
00:09:48,555 --> 00:09:49,141
meters.

269
00:09:49,321 --> 00:09:50,481
On a bad day,

270
00:09:50,481 --> 00:09:52,443
in broken rock or mud,

271
00:09:52,443 --> 00:09:54,940
it might not advance at all.

272
00:09:55,290 --> 00:09:57,774
Why did one of the biggest tunnel

273
00:09:57,774 --> 00:10:00,935
machines ever built stop under Seattle for

274
00:10:00,935 --> 00:10:01,688
two years?

275
00:10:01,868 --> 00:10:04,075
The machine, named Bertha,

276
00:10:04,075 --> 00:10:07,301
was more than seventeen meters across.

277
00:10:07,481 --> 00:10:10,005
In December two thousand thirteen,

278
00:10:10,005 --> 00:10:12,900
after about a thousand feet of digging,

279
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:14,830
it overheated and stopped.

280
00:10:15,010 --> 00:10:18,704
Investigators later found damaged seals around the

281
00:10:18,704 --> 00:10:21,363
main bearing, the part that lets the

282
00:10:21,363 --> 00:10:22,545
cutterhead turn.

283
00:10:22,894 --> 00:10:24,866
There was no way to reach the

284
00:10:24,866 --> 00:10:27,109
front of the machine from inside.

285
00:10:27,289 --> 00:10:29,574
So the builders dug a huge rescue

286
00:10:29,574 --> 00:10:31,028
pit from the surface,

287
00:10:31,028 --> 00:10:32,621
down to the cutterhead,

288
00:10:32,621 --> 00:10:34,767
and lifted the front end of the

289
00:10:34,767 --> 00:10:36,914
machine out with a giant crane.

290
00:10:37,094 --> 00:10:38,358
It was repaired,

291
00:10:38,358 --> 00:10:41,045
put back, and in late two thousand

292
00:10:41,045 --> 00:10:43,653
fifteen it started digging again.

293
00:10:44,003 --> 00:10:47,014
Bertha finished the tunnel in two thousand

294
00:10:47,014 --> 00:10:49,881
seventeen, and the new highway opened in

295
00:10:49,881 --> 00:10:51,458
two thousand nineteen,

296
00:10:51,458 --> 00:10:52,247
years late.

297
00:10:52,427 --> 00:10:55,505
Who was responsible became a long legal

298
00:10:55,505 --> 00:10:55,979
fight.

299
00:10:56,159 --> 00:10:58,558
The main bearing and its seals are

300
00:10:58,558 --> 00:11:00,323
the heart of the machine,

301
00:11:00,323 --> 00:11:02,934
and when the heart fails underground,

302
00:11:02,934 --> 00:11:04,980
there is no easy way to reach

303
00:11:04,980 --> 00:11:05,192
it.

304
00:11:05,541 --> 00:11:08,585
Why do tunnel boring machines have names

305
00:11:08,585 --> 00:11:11,172
like Bertha, Phyllis or Elizabeth?

306
00:11:11,352 --> 00:11:12,571
It is a tradition.

307
00:11:12,751 --> 00:11:15,645
Miners and tunnelers have long believed a

308
00:11:15,645 --> 00:11:18,610
machine should not start digging without a

309
00:11:18,610 --> 00:11:18,963
name.

310
00:11:19,143 --> 00:11:21,458
The names are usually female,

311
00:11:21,458 --> 00:11:24,012
often in honor of Saint Barbara,

312
00:11:24,012 --> 00:11:27,285
the patron saint of miners and tunnelers,

313
00:11:27,285 --> 00:11:29,840
who is said to protect them from

314
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,356
danger underground.

315
00:11:31,706 --> 00:11:32,997
On many projects,

316
00:11:32,997 --> 00:11:35,577
a small statue of Saint Barbara is

317
00:11:35,577 --> 00:11:38,385
placed at the entrance of the tunnel,

318
00:11:38,385 --> 00:11:41,421
and the machines are blessed before they

319
00:11:41,421 --> 00:11:41,877
start.

320
00:11:42,057 --> 00:11:43,966
On Crossrail in London,

321
00:11:43,966 --> 00:11:47,452
the eight machines were named after famous

322
00:11:47,452 --> 00:11:49,776
women from London's history,

323
00:11:49,776 --> 00:11:51,519
chosen by the public.

324
00:11:51,868 --> 00:11:54,058
It sounds like superstition,

325
00:11:54,058 --> 00:11:54,841
and it is.

326
00:11:55,021 --> 00:11:57,243
But tunneling was one of the most

327
00:11:57,243 --> 00:12:00,071
dangerous jobs in the world for centuries.

328
00:12:00,251 --> 00:12:04,907
Collapses, floods and gas killed many workers.

329
00:12:05,087 --> 00:12:06,949
A name and a saint at the

330
00:12:06,949 --> 00:12:09,854
entrance are a reminder that the people

331
00:12:09,854 --> 00:12:12,610
inside are the most important part of

332
00:12:12,610 --> 00:12:13,504
the machine.

333
00:12:13,853 --> 00:12:16,561
How does a machine deep underground,

334
00:12:16,561 --> 00:12:17,464
with no GPS,

335
00:12:17,464 --> 00:12:19,194
know where it is going?

336
00:12:19,374 --> 00:12:20,965
It follows a laser.

337
00:12:21,145 --> 00:12:23,819
A survey station fixed to the tunnel

338
00:12:23,819 --> 00:12:26,641
wall behind the machine shoots a laser

339
00:12:26,641 --> 00:12:29,167
beam onto a target on the machine.

340
00:12:29,347 --> 00:12:32,888
Computers read the target and calculate the

341
00:12:32,888 --> 00:12:36,759
exact position and direction of the cutterhead,

342
00:12:36,759 --> 00:12:39,970
so the operator can correct the course.

343
00:12:40,320 --> 00:12:43,164
The survey station itself must be moved

344
00:12:43,164 --> 00:12:45,205
forward as the tunnel grows,

345
00:12:45,205 --> 00:12:47,611
and every move adds a tiny error.

346
00:12:47,791 --> 00:12:51,109
Surveyors check the line again and again,

347
00:12:51,109 --> 00:12:54,589
using points measured from the surface down

348
00:12:54,589 --> 00:12:55,802
through shafts.

349
00:12:55,982 --> 00:12:58,105
In a tunnel kilometers long,

350
00:12:58,105 --> 00:13:00,228
being off by a fraction of a

351
00:13:00,228 --> 00:13:03,184
degree would mean missing the target by

352
00:13:03,184 --> 00:13:03,715
meters.

353
00:13:04,064 --> 00:13:05,309
Under the Channel,

354
00:13:05,309 --> 00:13:08,282
the British and French tunnels started more

355
00:13:08,282 --> 00:13:10,287
than thirty kilometers apart.

356
00:13:10,467 --> 00:13:13,567
When they met in December nineteen ninety,

357
00:13:13,567 --> 00:13:17,110
the difference was about thirty five centimeters

358
00:13:17,110 --> 00:13:20,136
sideways and less than six centimeters up

359
00:13:20,136 --> 00:13:20,800
and down.

360
00:13:20,980 --> 00:13:23,987
Two tunnels dug from two countries,

361
00:13:23,987 --> 00:13:25,190
under the sea,

362
00:13:25,190 --> 00:13:26,994
met almost perfectly.

363
00:13:27,344 --> 00:13:29,884
Why did the Gotthard Base Tunnel in

364
00:13:29,884 --> 00:13:33,295
Switzerland need four giant machines working at

365
00:13:33,295 --> 00:13:34,310
the same time?

366
00:13:34,490 --> 00:13:37,737
Because it is fifty seven kilometers long,

367
00:13:37,737 --> 00:13:40,829
the longest railway tunnel in the world.

368
00:13:41,009 --> 00:13:43,670
One machine would have taken far too

369
00:13:43,670 --> 00:13:44,040
long.

370
00:13:44,220 --> 00:13:46,785
So the builders dug access shafts and

371
00:13:46,785 --> 00:13:48,588
tunnels into the mountain,

372
00:13:48,588 --> 00:13:51,638
and the machines started from several points

373
00:13:51,638 --> 00:13:54,065
at once, digging toward each other.

374
00:13:54,414 --> 00:13:55,493
Under the Alps,

375
00:13:55,493 --> 00:13:58,297
up to two thousand three hundred meters

376
00:13:58,297 --> 00:14:00,382
of rock sit above the tunnel.

377
00:14:00,562 --> 00:14:02,252
The rock there is hot,

378
00:14:02,252 --> 00:14:04,557
sometimes above forty degrees,

379
00:14:04,557 --> 00:14:06,708
and it can squeeze in on the

380
00:14:06,708 --> 00:14:09,397
tunnel or burst out from the walls.

381
00:14:09,577 --> 00:14:12,218
The machines had to dig through granite

382
00:14:12,218 --> 00:14:14,790
and gneiss, and some sections were dug

383
00:14:14,790 --> 00:14:16,415
with explosives instead.

384
00:14:16,765 --> 00:14:20,148
The tunnel opened in two thousand sixteen,

385
00:14:20,148 --> 00:14:23,209
after seventeen years of construction.

386
00:14:23,389 --> 00:14:26,413
Freight trains now cross the Alps almost

387
00:14:26,413 --> 00:14:29,589
flat, without climbing over the mountains.

388
00:14:29,769 --> 00:14:30,814
And the machines?

389
00:14:30,994 --> 00:14:33,717
They were taken apart and carried out,

390
00:14:33,717 --> 00:14:34,792
piece by piece,

391
00:14:34,792 --> 00:14:36,799
to dig again somewhere else.

392
00:14:37,148 --> 00:14:39,296
So here are the eleven.

393
00:14:39,476 --> 00:14:41,914
A machine that cannot reverse,

394
00:14:41,914 --> 00:14:44,027
so it is sometimes buried.

395
00:14:44,207 --> 00:14:46,800
Steel discs that let the rock break

396
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:47,319
itself.

397
00:14:47,499 --> 00:14:50,660
Concrete puzzle pieces locked by a key,

398
00:14:50,660 --> 00:14:53,173
jacks that push off the tunnel,

399
00:14:53,173 --> 00:14:55,766
and grout that holds up the city

400
00:14:55,766 --> 00:14:56,253
above.

401
00:14:56,433 --> 00:14:59,288
A chamber of mud that balances the

402
00:14:59,288 --> 00:15:02,479
ground, a train of trailers behind the

403
00:15:02,479 --> 00:15:05,670
cutter, a bearing that stopped Bertha,

404
00:15:05,670 --> 00:15:07,266
a name and a saint,

405
00:15:07,266 --> 00:15:08,946
a laser that steers,

406
00:15:08,946 --> 00:15:11,549
and four giants under the Alps.

407
00:15:11,898 --> 00:15:14,134
Next time you ride a subway or

408
00:15:14,134 --> 00:15:15,847
drive through a tunnel,

409
00:15:15,847 --> 00:15:18,753
remember that a machine like this built

410
00:15:18,753 --> 00:15:20,019
it, ring by ring,

411
00:15:20,019 --> 00:15:22,255
and might still be down there.

412
00:15:22,435 --> 00:15:25,400
If you want more machines explained part

413
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:28,216
by part, subscribe to Machine Reverse.

414
00:15:28,396 --> 00:15:30,425
And tell us in the comments which

415
00:15:30,425 --> 00:15:32,454
machine you want us to take apart

416
00:15:32,454 --> 00:15:32,762
next.
